<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Solomon Islands</h1><p class="st-meta">1978 CE – present · capital Honiara</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Melanesian archipelago state, independent from Britain in 1978, shaken by ethnic violence from 1998 to 2003 and an Australian-led intervention.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The southern islands became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-solomon-islands-protectorate">British Solomon Islands Protectorate</a> in June 1893. The administration was renamed the Solomon Islands in 1975, self-government came in 1976, and independence on 7 July 1978 under Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-kenilorea">Peter Kenilorea</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a> as queen.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Governments have changed often by votes of no confidence; Solomon Mamaloni dominated politics in the early to mid 1990s. From 1998 the Isatabu Freedom Movement on Guadalcanal and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaita">Malaita</a> Eagle Force fought, and about 200 people were killed; the economy collapsed and the government went bankrupt. The Townsville Peace Agreement was signed on 15 October 2000. In July 2003 the Regional Assistance Mission to Solomon Islands, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, arrived, and it withdrew in 2017. Land is generally held by families or villages.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>In 2019 the government switched its recognition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and in April 2022 it signed a security agreement with China that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> said it would be concerned by. Rioting in November 2021 brought an Australian deployment. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeremiah">Jeremiah</a> Manele was elected prime minister in May 2024, succeeding Manasseh Sogavare. Charles III is king, represented by a governor-general.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/solomon-islands.jpg" alt="Solomon Islands" width="330" height="165"><figcaption>Image: User:SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Solomon_Islands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1978 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Honiara</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Peter Kenilorea, Solomon Mamaloni, Manasseh Sogavare, Allan Kemakeza, Jeremiah Manele</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christian denominations: Anglican, Catholic, South Seas Evangelical Church, United Church, Seventh-day Adventist</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Solomons Pijin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Solomon Islands</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2002&amp;focus=solomon-islands" aria-label="Solomon Islands on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="9094 2530 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Solomon Islands lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M9414 2786L9422 2773"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M9422 2773h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M9414 2786h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M9414 2786h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Honiara. <a href="/atlas?year=2002&amp;focus=solomon-islands">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
